---
import Page from "../layouts/Page.astro";
import PageHeader from "@oblivios/site-shared/components/PageHeader.astro";
import Print from "@oblivios/site-shared/components/Print.astro";
import { sources } from "../lib/site.mjs";

sources("privacy", ["Design of the public launch, sections 5.4 and 5.5", "README.md", "site/org/vercel.json (the headers listed on the page)", "Vercel's documentation of Web Analytics (vercel.com/docs/analytics and /docs/analytics/privacy-policy), read on 2026-11-18: no cookies, a visitor is a daily hash, the data points", "Decision 261 (the owner wants the analytics to see whether there is interest)"]);
---
<Page
  title="Privacy: ObliviOS"
  description="This site sets no cookies and makes no request to other hosts. It counts page views with Vercel Web Analytics, without cookies. Vercel serves it and sees IP addresses."
  path="/privacy"
>
  <PageHeader crumb="Privacy" title="Privacy" lede="What this site does with your visit, and what the phone system collects.">
    <Print id="e" label="An eye, crossed out" />
  </PageHeader>
  <section class="band">
    <div class="c">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>This site</h2>
        <p>This site is a set of static files. It sets no cookie and uses no local storage. Its fonts, images and icons are served from the site itself, so your browser makes no request to any other host.</p>
        <p>I count page views with Vercel Web Analytics, to see whether anyone is interested. One small script, served from this site's own address, sends each page view to Vercel without a cookie: the address of the page, the page you came from, your country, and your browser, system and device type. Vercel says that the data is anonymous and not tied to an IP address, and that a visitor is a hash that is discarded after 24 hours. There is no other script and no other analytics. Each page also carries a block of structured data for search engines (schema.org, as JSON): it is plain data, runs nothing and contacts nobody.</p>
        <p>It sends these headers on every page: <code>Strict-Transport-Security</code>, a <code>Content-Security-Policy</code> that allows only the site's own address and forbids forms, a base address and framing by other sites, <code>Referrer-Policy: no-referrer</code>, <code>X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff</code>, <code>X-Frame-Options</code>, a <code>Permissions-Policy</code> that switches off the camera, the microphone, the location, the motion sensors and the other device interfaces, and <code>Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy</code>. Vercel adds <code>Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *</code> to the static files; the site holds nothing that is not public.</p>
        <h2>Who serves it</h2>
        <p>The site is hosted on Vercel, a third party. Vercel serves the pages and sees your IP address in its logs. I turn Vercel's speed insights and toolbar off. Because the output is static, it can move to any other host, and I keep the domain names under my own control.</p>
        <p>The links to the repositories lead to GitHub. When you follow one, GitHub's own terms apply.</p>
        <h2>The phone system</h2>
        <p>The constraints are written in the README: no ObliviOS account, no mandatory telemetry, no advertising identifier and no cloud dependency. The Passwords app has no network permission. Anything a feature sends elsewhere is a choice you make per app, such as sending an app's traffic through Tor.</p>
        <div class="callout"><h3>How to check the site</h3><p>Open the network panel of your browser and load any page. You should see requests to this site only, among them one small request for the page view, and no <code>Set-Cookie</code> header.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Page>
